WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.602
-Hey!
Thanks so much for doing this.

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.604
It is great to see you.
-Great to see you.

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.406
-I want to talk to you about
a couple cool things

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:07.774
that you're doing.

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:09.241
One you did during quarantine.

00:00:09.241 --> 00:00:11.678
You taught a Broadway
Zoom class.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.446
-Yeah.
-What was that like?

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:14.747
And how did you get involved?

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:17.116
And had you ever taught
a class before?

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:20.119
-Well, first of all, I'd never
taught a class before,

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:22.222
and I was -- I think I was
way more terrified

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:24.223
than any of the students,
way more nervous.

00:00:24.223 --> 00:00:26.425
But my friend actually created
this platform.

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:28.661
It's amazing.
It's called "Broadway Weekend."

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:32.165
And it's where Broadway
and West End professionals

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:34.534
teach theater classes
whenever their schools

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:35.734
are online.

00:00:35.734 --> 00:00:37.970
And it's created this like
global community.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.039
And it's great because,
you know, when these performers

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:41.607
can't perform
'cause of lockdown

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:43.476
they're getting to share
their skills

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:45.645
and people at home
can be creative

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:48.247
and learn and try
and have some fun.

00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:49.682
-That's a great idea.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.584
-I really recommend everyone
to check it out.

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:53.786
-Once you did it,
did you feel better at it,

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:55.755
once you started doing it?

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.591
-I mean, I was teaching
audition technique,

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.259
which is completely insane

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:03.396
because I am
terrible at auditions.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:06.900
-I know that you audition
for "Mamma Mia" was a --

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.536
It ended up being a big weekend
for you.

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:13.406
'Cause -- Was it Glastonbury?
-Yeah.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.041
God. I mean, it was insane.

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.943
I knew I had to sing
in this audition.

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.510
I was desperate for this part.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.980
But I was meant to be scheduled

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.648
at the end of Glastonbury
Music Festival

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:24.783
and I knew for sure that
after screaming in a field

00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:27.653
for five days, there was no way
I'd be able to sing.

00:01:27.653 --> 00:01:30.956
-Oh, my gosh.
-So I did it on the way.

00:01:30.956 --> 00:01:33.726
I had the car packed with the
stuff and the whirlies

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:37.763
and the outfits,
and then I went in, I sang,

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:39.632
went to Glastonbury

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:41.534
and my phone ran out
of battery,

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.903
and I think I found out --
I think they tried to

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:45.538
call me and tell me
I got the part,

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.307
and it wasn't until I was
in the car on the way home

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:49.509
I plugged in my phone
and I listened --

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:51.144
and my agent calls me
and I'm like,

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:52.778
[ Hoarse voice ]
"Hello?"

00:01:52.778 --> 00:01:54.480
[ Laughter ]
"You got the job."

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.983
I'm like, "Please say
it doesn't start soon."

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.118
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:00.887
-You're the lead
in "Mamma Mia 2."

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:02.622
You're like, [ Hoarse voice ]
"Oh, this is fantastic.

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.924
Oh, God."
-"Great news."

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.893
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:08.261
"Thank you."

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:10.596
-Speaking of "Mamma Mia,"
this summer the producers

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:13.265
actually said that it was
meant to be a trilogy.

00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:16.635
So there may be a "Mamma Mia 3"
in the works.

00:02:16.635 --> 00:02:19.706
Would you be interested
if that --

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:21.307
-Yeah.
I'd want to do like

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:22.975
"4," "5," "6," "7,"
"8," "9," "10."

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:26.245
I'm down. I'm like, signed,
sealed, delivered on those.

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:28.714
-Yeah. Hey, I want to talk
about "Rebecca,"

00:02:28.714 --> 00:02:31.350
your new movie which is based on
the famous book.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.853
It's part love story, it's
part thriller, part mystery.

00:02:34.853 --> 00:02:36.588
Can you set up what it's about?

00:02:36.588 --> 00:02:38.491
-It's based on the book
by Daphne du Maurier.

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:40.126
It's the most wonderful,
wonderful book.

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:41.928
And it starts as this
sweeping romance

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:44.864
and then you follow this
newlywed, who I play.

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:48.067
She follows her husband to
this -- back to England

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.670
to this huge, imposing estate
called Mandley,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.605
which is almost a character
in itself,

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:54.740
and this story sort of
flip reverses

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.843
into a gothic horror
ghost story

00:02:57.843 --> 00:03:00.445
and it becomes just terrifying.

00:03:00.445 --> 00:03:01.881
It's got a great cast.

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:04.683
Kristen Scott Thomas plays
Mrs. Danvers and she's just --

00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:07.153
-Come on.
-Oh, just chilling

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.422
and wonderful in every way.

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:10.723
-I love that you got her --

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:12.358
I mean, that someone
got her to do this.

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:14.627
'Cause I thought she was only
going to do theater.

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.762
-She's honestly --
I just adore her.

00:03:16.762 --> 00:03:18.130
-Yeah.
-And she's so --

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.633
She flips the part as well.
It kind of becomes --

00:03:21.633 --> 00:03:24.069
because Hitchcock made this film
too very famously,

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:26.072
and she flips this part
from the villain to almost

00:03:26.072 --> 00:03:29.175
the moral compass from the film.
-She's brilliant.

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:31.678
And Ben Wheatley directed it,
and he also --

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:35.948
he's so good at delving into the
darkness of our human psyche.

00:03:35.948 --> 00:03:38.851
And so it's a psychological
thriller, really.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.486
-Yeah. Yeah, have you ever had

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:43.522
any experiences with
ghosts yourself?

00:03:43.522 --> 00:03:44.924
-You know what?

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.659
I just figured something out

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.628
and I'm a bit traumatized by it,

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:51.064
which is that my whole life

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:52.999
I've had these
reoccurring nightmares

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.067
where a dark figure
walks towards me

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:56.402
and like leans over me.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.971
And I wake up screaming.

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:01.073
And it goes into
my consciousness,

00:04:01.073 --> 00:04:03.109
and whoever I've been with
will be like,

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:04.677
"It's okay, Lily.
It's okay, Lily."

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:07.647
I was in a dorm
for nearly ten years

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.916
with one girl
when I was at school,

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:11.651
and she'd comfort me
once a week.

00:04:11.651 --> 00:04:13.486
Always once a week,
I'd wake up screaming.

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:17.690
And I had only just realized
this, but her name is Rebecca.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.925
-No. You're kidding.

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:21.360
-No, I'm not kidding.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.730
So I think she's been screwing
with me my entire life.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:26.699
-Oh, my gosh.

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:28.734
Dude, now the dreams are going
to get crazier and crazier.

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:30.102
No, they won't.
I'm going to put it out there

00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:31.504
in the world right now.
They're gone.

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:32.705
Yeah. I will --
-Okay, good.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:33.906
-Yeah, I'll talk to somebody.

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:35.508
I'll make sure it's all good.
-Can you please?

00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:37.042
-I do want to show everyone
a clip, though.

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:40.279
Here are Lily James and
Armie Hammer in "Rebecca."

00:04:40.279 --> 00:04:41.647
Take a look.

00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:43.582
-Bonjour.
-Mademoiselle.

00:04:43.582 --> 00:04:46.252
Ah. Madam Van Hopper's
usual table.

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.654
-Oh, yes, thank you, merci.
Oh, no.

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:50.823
Wait. Sorry.
She was wondering

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.992
if a Mr. De Winter
could sit with her.

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:54.494
-Monsieur Maxim De Winter?

00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:55.661
-Oui.

00:04:55.661 --> 00:04:57.163
-It is the choice
of Mr. De Winter

00:04:57.163 --> 00:04:59.231
where he wishes to be seated.
-Oh, yes.

00:04:59.231 --> 00:05:02.835
Of course.
Merci.

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:04.937
So sorry.
Is that not good enough?

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:06.539
Here, I've got more.

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:08.241
[ Coins dropping ]

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:11.144
God, I'm so sorry, monsieur.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:13.312
Just one moment.
-Don't do it.

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:16.315
I've heard he's a terrible bore.
-Excuse me?

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:19.085
-This De Winter chap.
-Thank you.

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:23.890
-Monsieur De Winter,
please, this way.

00:05:26.525 --> 00:05:28.727
-Lily James.
"Rebecca" is streaming

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:31.664
on Netflix and in select
theaters right now.

